 | D-110 | 2026-11-14 | A cancelled message counts as a failure of its flow (architect, on the owner's own principle; the owner did not answer this one). It stays in the error rate, in error alerting and in whatever counts failures, and it is not a success the way D-105's `handled` is. | Derived from the owner's reasoning for D-105 rather than decided afresh: a handled failure is a success because the flow dealt with it and the message reached its destination, and a cancelled message is the opposite — the operator abandoned it and it was never delivered. The business outcome is a non-delivery, so calling it anything but a failure would turn cancel into a button that makes a chart green. Recorded as mine, not the owner's: it was put to the owner together with the sync question on 2026-11-14 and the answer covered only resending. Review at hand-over. Sharpened by the owner the same day ("zrušená zpráva bude vidět v logu jako zrušená (třeba kvůli chybě)"): the two things are separate and must not be conflated. The message's STATE is `cancelled`, a state of its own, shown as such on its screen with the reason the operator gave — never flattened into `failed`, which is what D-108's derived state and the `reason` column on the cancel event already provide. What this row decides is only the COUNTING: for the flow's error rate a cancelled message is a failure, because it was never delivered. If the owner wants cancelled to be a third category in the counting too — neither error nor success — that is a one-line change here and in CONSIDER-11d's `handled` number. |
