 | D-112 | 2026-11-14 | `BatchingWriter.takeLocked`'s outer loop stays a `while`, not an `if`, although a second pass is unreachable today (FIX-137, reversible). The unreachability is a real invariant and is now written into the javadoc: at rest either `queue.size() < batchSize` or `inFlight == commitConcurrency`, and waiters exist only when the queue is full, which implies the latter. Entered with every commit slot taken the condition is false at once; entered with at most `batchSize` queued - `enqueue` can reach exactly `batchSize`, which is why the ENTRY case says "at most" where the AT-REST invariant says "fewer than" - the first pass takes the whole queue and nothing refills it; entered from `afterCommit` with a slot just freed, the one batch retakes that slot, so the condition is false whatever the waiters refilled. A drain therefore takes at most one batch. The loop is kept anyway. | **REASON CORRECTED 2026-11-14 by FIX-137's reviewer; the first version of this cell was wrong.** It said an `if` would leave items queued with no commit in flight and no window armed - "a silent stall, which is the one failure this class must not have". That is false, and it contradicted a sentence already four lines above in the same file: `drainLocked`'s own javadoc says "nothing stays queued with no commit in flight and no window" and "when nothing was taken the queue is non-empty, so a commit is already in flight and its completion drains". A take always does `inFlight++`, and that commit's completion re-enters `afterCommit` -> `drainLocked`, so an `if` would be CORRECT. The real cost of an `if` is latency and throughput: leftovers wait for the next completion or for the window, one batch per event, and a waiter can reach `maxWait`. That is why the `while` is kept - to avoid that latency if the invariant ever breaks - and the one equivalent mutant it leaves (the loop's repeat) is accepted. The reviewer also notes PIT has no while-to-if mutator, so "the loop's repeat" may not 