+| D-113 | 2026-11-14 | A catch clause's width follows from what the clause DOES, and the two kinds are: a clause that TRANSLATES a synchronous throw into a value the caller already handles - a failed future, an HTTP status, a logged drop - takes `Throwable`, because the caller handles every throwable equally well through that value and a narrower clause instead produces a hang, a lost reply or an abandoned loop; a clause that DECIDES POLICY - retry this, park this, call this a validation problem - stays as narrow as the policy, because an `Error` is not a transient failure, not a parkable failure and not a problem with the thing being validated, and widening such a clause would misclassify a broken JVM as a broken flow. FIX-143 triaged ten clauses by this rule: eight RIGHT, two WRONG (FIX-149, FIX-150). | Reversible, and it settles a question that had been re-asked at every site since F-30. The rule was already implicit in FIX-136's packet and is now explicit, because reading the same ten clauses without it produced three different counts and one clause that D-111 had already settled being put back on the list. Worked through: `Deployments:949` and `:1482` are policy clauses turning a refusal into a problem line, and an `Error` out of either is contained one frame out by the deliberate clauses at `:826` (`Throwable`) or `:927` (`RuntimeException` or `Error`) - comment, no change. `:1055`, `:1278`, `:1321`, `:1392` are the same policy kind; on the deploy path `:826` contains an `Error`, and on the validate path `validate` catches only `DeployRefusedException` so it propagates out of `Deployments` - which is RIGHT, because aborting a validation is the intended answer to a broken node, where a problem line saying "this flow does not build: OutOfMemoryError" would be a lie about the flow. `AdminServer:597` is narrow DELIBERATELY and says so - its javadoc reads "an Error is the caller's (500)" and `run()` catches `Throwable` one frame out. `Retry:44` likewise: the class